\section*{The exact three-state reconstruction threshold including equality}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Three-state symmetric broadcasting is claimed reconstructible precisely when $d\lambda^2>1$ on regular and observed Poisson trees, for both signs of the channel parameter.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The posterior is represented by a complex mean and two invariant statistics. Fixed-point inequalities separately exclude positive and negative threshold reconstruction, and an exact finite certificate treats the coloring boundary; known transfer theorems then give the corresponding three-community graph recovery threshold.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Three-state symmetric broadcasting is claimed reconstructible precisely when $d\lambda^2>1$ on regular and observed Poisson trees, for both signs of the channel parameter. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 229, the three-state Comparator entries only establish reconstruction when $d\lambda^2>1$. They omit threshold nonreconstruction, the graph recovery corollary, and the four-state companions. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{ThreeStateSupercritical.lean}, \texttt{ThreeStateTreeClauses.lean}. The inventory does not supply a named declaration for these Comparator entries. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

